
+ Development approach ...

  + Edit, build, view locally.
  + Build in docker container ...

    make docker

  + Commit edits that work
  + Push to github (auto publish in github pages)

  + Release on github major milestones (e.g., version 1)
    + Change version in VERSION file
    + Rebuild (should update version info in HTML output + Docker image)
    + git tag with version
    + git commit (including tag)
    + docker push image
    + github release


+ Reproducibility
  
  + Use renv::init() to auto capture packages used in book
    + Local builds will use frozen packages via {renv} library
  + Subsequently supplement with install.packages() (auto captured by {renv})
    + Going forward, new packages should be auto captured
  + Use renv::snapshot(type="all") to capture packages and versions in renv.lock
    + Re-run after new package install to update renv.lock
      BUT ONLY after checking local build produces right result
  + Base Dockerfile on renv.lock with call to renv::restore()
    + Updates to renv.lock should trigger docker image rebuild
      (quite hefty because it will install ALL packages all over again)
  + 'make docker' just points R_LIBS to {renv} library cache 
    (does NOT rely on renv::activate())


+ Regression testing

  + Part of 'make docker' now does a {gdiff} check on all book figures
    and should report any changes

  + NOTE that model images for any new image directories or new chapters will
    need to be manually added to ...

    ./gdiff/


+ NOTE that with modern browsers need to run a local server to 
  view local files (to avoid CORS errors)

  npx http-server docs -o -p 9999 

  Ctrl-C to shut down, or if running in bg use this to find process id

  lsof -i :9999


+ NOTE that building in Ubuntu 24.04 (with Cairo 1.18.0) instead
  of Ubuntu 20.04 (with Cairo 1.16.0) generates some SVG figures (for HTML)
  that Firefox and Chrome cannot render (although eog can).
  The trigger appears to be including a raster image in the SVG
  (e.g., a colour gradient colorbar legend OR a raster icon).
  This is why the Docker build is based on Ubuntu 22.04
  (22.04 rather than 20.04 because some R packages are getting hard to install
   on 20.04).

  The raster legend can be worked around with 
  guide_colorbar(display="rectangles"), but explicit rasters are harder.
  Another workaround would be to generate PNG rather than SVG (for HTML),
  but that would produce lower quality images.


